  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "On the left side of the graph, we see that as the values of $x$ decrease and approach $-\\infty $, the graph of the function moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=3.$ \n\nConsequently, $f(x)$ approaches the value $3$ as $x$ approaches $-\\infty ,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=3.\n$$\n\nOn the right side of the graph, as the values of $x$ increase and approach $\\infty ,$ the graph of the function goes without bound to $-\\infty .$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=-\\infty .\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
